cast:
  Niamh:
  Zofia:
  Morwenna:
  Grace:
  Hale:
  Kit:
  Hugh:
  Darwin:
  Mara:
  Len:
~~~~

give_it_back
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_give_it_back_place">In the Palace waiting room, Zofia’s fingers cover the edge of Niamh’s envelope.</Markdown>
::
Niamh: My mum is in Belfast. Her letter has come back from London with three attachments and no answer. Give me the original.
Zofia: The address. I can change that and take it with Hale's—
Niamh: You can give it to me.
Morwenna: I checked the heading, not who held the record. And I broke my promise by telling Ruth about us. Checking the other details didn’t put either right.
Zofia: You thought we were in trouble.
Morwenna: We were. I still broke the promise.
Niamh: My mother signed for me to help her. She didn't sign for everyone in this room to pass her papers round.
Zofia: I haven't shown them to everyone.
Grace: You're doing the holding-on thing. With your actual hands.
Zofia: Oh.
Niamh: Thank you. The receipt names her care trust. I'll ring its records team and ask where her correction request belongs. If they say somewhere else, I want that address in writing.
Morwenna: Health and social care are devolved in Northern Ireland. A UK label didn't make our proposed government-history Bill the right route for this record. I should have said that before you lost the appointment.
Niamh: And the Assembly isn't my mother's filing cabinet. I need the body holding her record, not another impressive building.
Zofia: Will you tell me what they say?
Niamh: If Mum wants you told.
Grace: Can you live with that?
Zofia: I can try. Hale's waiting, and I can't take him down a telephone.
Niamh: Then take him where you promised. I'll take Mum's letter.

which_house
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_which_house_place">In the Lords chamber, their escort stops them short of the Woolsack.</Markdown>
::
Hale: If this rich seat commands their Commons, madam, let our petition be laid before its officer; substance should follow authority. [class=dead]
Zofia: He thinks the cushion is where we take the Commons papers.
Morwenna: No. This is the House of Lords. The Woolsack is the Lord Speaker's seat, not the Commons Speaker's chair. Please don't take anything forward. Our escort hasn't asked us to.
Hale: If it be another House, I withdraw that inference; tell the young gentlewoman I would have its office named before its judgment sought. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says name the office first. He's dropped the Commons bit.
Kit: Lord Speaker. Lords. Stop, check—sorry. I nearly saluted it.
Grace: Does it salute back?
Kit: I was moving my hand. It wasn't a completed salute.
Morwenna: Put it in your pocket before it becomes one.
Grace: A cushion. But why do we still do this?
Hugh: The wool recalls the wealth built on England's medieval wool trade. I would keep that material reminder of where power came from, because polished words can conceal it rather easily.
Grace: My dad's wages don't get a cushion. They get a rota.
Hugh: Then keep the rota as well. I have had recent cause to regret a history which remembered the important man and lost the worker behind him.
Morwenna: And I need us to remember which House we're asking. Otherwise I put another person's letter under another wrong heading.
Grace: All right. Keep the cushion. Who pays for the person checking the heading?
Zofia: Not Niamh. Not again.
Grace: That's one answer. It still isn't a wage.

keep_damage
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_keep_damage_place">Back in the waiting room, Hugh lays Hale’s disputed summary on the table.</Markdown>
::
Hale: If the false account remains, Master Mercer, its injury remains; strike out the charge that my refusal confessed disloyalty. [class=dead]
Zofia: He wants the summary deleted. I think I do too. People can still read it while we wait.
Hugh: They can. But remember the accusation finally removed from the Monument in 1830. Knowing what was cut away matters. If we preserve only the clean version, we lose evidence that an authority once put the accusation there.
Zofia: The missing line. We needed the record of that as well as the stone.
Hugh: Exactly. I would retain the summary with the correction, its evidence and author beside it.
Zofia: Hale, he wants your reason beside their claim, where nobody can pretend it was always there.
Hale: If the clerk records my conscience only as another accusation, I gain no remedy; require him to distinguish what I said from what his predecessor inferred. [class=dead]
Zofia: That's what he's asking now. His reason, not a certificate saying every belief he held was right.
Hugh: He is right about the present harm. Preserving an unannotated error continues to lend our authority to it. I should have said so sooner.
Morwenna: The Historical Accounts Office has sent something. Shall I open it?
Zofia: Yes. Please.
Morwenna: “Application retained pending policy decision. No determination of the evidence has been made.”
Hale: If no evidence has been weighed, no judgment has been given; madam, do not enter this delay as my answer. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says that's a delay. He's still here.
Hugh: Then the open-claims list must still contain him. The Workshop cannot amend the government's source account merely by changing its reproduction.
Zofia: I wanted that envelope to be the last one.
Morwenna: So did I. I'll leave the box open.

unfinished
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_unfinished_place">At the campaign-room table, Zofia pins the animal-review form beneath her mug.</Markdown>
::
Darwin: The horse's endurance may show that it endured, Miss Kowalczyk. I do not think it tells us whether it could refuse. What separate observation would support that conclusion? [class=dead]
Zofia: He says staying under a load doesn't tell us it chose the load. But the dog follows. Couldn't that one want to?
Grace: It could want its handler. Does that mean it agreed to everything the handler made it do?
Zofia: No. Ugh. That's what I was doing.
Darwin: You felt the dog follow a departing footstep. It may have sought its handler; it may have learnt that remaining behind brought punishment. Can you yet distinguish those explanations? [class=dead]
Zofia: It might be scared of staying. I hadn’t thought of that.
Hugh: Zofia, the Workshop editor has met the minister’s recorded deadline. The signed reply is here, with the published correction.
Zofia: Now? We're in the middle of—yes. Give it here.
Grace: Do you want a minute?
Zofia: If I wait, I'm only making him stay to help me.
Darwin: Wallace’s paper first, if you please. What have they printed beside mine? [class=dead]

::
<Markdown id="uk_10_editor_response">
**Witness Workshop — signed response from the editor**
We withdraw “Darwin alone”. The record of the joint reading at the Linnean Society on 1 July 1858, arranged by Lyell and Hooker, establishes Alfred Russel Wallace’s part. Our published correction names both men; the old caption remains beside it.
</Markdown>
::

Zofia: Mr Darwin, I’m reading their letter. “We withdraw ‘Darwin alone’.” Because of the joint reading, yours and Wallace’s, on 1 July 1858. They’ve named you both. It’s on the page now, beside the old one.
Zofia: Did you want his name first? Mr Darwin?
Grace: Zof?
Zofia: I still need to ask him about the dog.
Grace: Put the page down. I'll move the mug.
Zofia: I haven't finished the form.
Grace: Neither have I. Let me see.
Zofia: The box. It says “resolved” once we've put a reviewer's name in. I was about to tick it.
Grace: Have they answered the voluntary-service claim?
Zofia: No. They've accepted a review.
Grace: Then cross that word out.
Zofia: Done. Open. I can write that much.
Grace: Give me the pen.

open_claims
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_open_claims_place">At the same table, Mara draws three boxes on a fresh sheet.</Markdown>
::
Mara: Zofia, I’ll get these answered before the door closes. Give me the list.
Grace: Will you? Hale’s letter says they haven’t even weighed the evidence.
Mara: Then write “pursue a response” above my signature. Leave their answer blank.
Len: Answer, miss. Beg pardon, but an address for it would help. The Army managed to find me when it wanted me. [class=dead]
Zofia: Len wants a reply address attached, not just his name on a list.
Mara: His own entry: HAO unit account, neutral-purpose wording, our evidence, policy hold. I undertake to pursue an evidence-based response and the statutory route to require one.
Len: Required, miss. That sounds more like it. Only don't write “received” where you mean “sent”. We had enough of that on the forms. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says don't confuse sending it with getting it. Put the reply field underneath.
Mara: Yes. Hale next. I undertake to pursue a response recording his stated reason and examining the inference of disloyalty, with evidence and reasons. No promise to reverse history.
Hale: If reasons be required, madam, require their author also; an unsigned refusal leaves no man bound to defend it. [class=dead]
Zofia: He wants the person responsible named. Can we ask for that?
Mara: We can ask, and make clear who holds the account. I will pursue that too.
Zofia: And the animals. Don't combine their case with the other two.
Mara: The Workshop’s voluntary-service claim remains disputed. A reviewer named; no answer. I undertake to pursue that review and its reasons. Our representation clause lets a living applicant bring a claim; it doesn’t decide its truth.
Grace: Or make an animal sign something it can't read.
Mara: Correct. I'll sign these undertakings and put them on the campaign's public record today. Separate entries. Separate follow-up.
Zofia: I'll tell them what you've signed. Only that.
Len: Signed, miss. Good. Now keep a copy where the orderly can't sit on it. [class=dead]
Hale: If the undertaking be all we have, keep its terms exact; hope is a poor clerk, madam. [class=dead]

ask_admission
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_ask_admission_place">Beside the campaign-room door, Kit holds his cue card against the frame.</Markdown>
::
Grace: But why do we still do this? Hugh, you get to defend that door from inside. If it shuts you out, is it still yours to defend?
Kit: The summons has to ask admission. Door, knocks, admission. I have one support cue. Knowing the symbolism won’t help if I miss it.
Hugh: The closed door asserts the Commons’ independence from the Crown. That is worth defending. Whether we have made its independence serve you is a harder question.
Grace: So a door is a veto?
Zofia: A veto. Like liberum veto in the Commonwealth Sejm. The Constitution of 3 May 1791 abolished it as an obstruction to government. A rule can protect somebody and still stop everyone else getting anything done.
Grace: Is that what this door does?
Zofia: No. They admit Black Rod after the knocks. The summons goes on. I wasn't saying it's the same rule.
Grace: You sounded pleased with the comparison for a second.
Zofia: I was. It was quite a good second.
Kit: We don't get an extra set of knocks because the first set meant something sad. My cue isn't permission to start it all again.
Zofia: I know.
Grace: You keep asking how long the gap is.
Zofia: Because I need to tell them three things. Len, Hale, the animals. Properly.
Kit: Then tell them beforehand as well. I'll help you practise the words. I won't help you make the gap longer.
Zofia: You could leave me a space on the card.
Grace: Does the comparison save them, Zof?
Zofia: No.
Grace: Then don't let a good argument use up the time we actually have.

no_second_cue
---
::
<Markdown id="uk_10_no_second_cue_place">At the campaign-room table, Kit slides the corrected packet into its pocket.</Markdown>
::
Kit: Crosses, quarters, supporters. Lords for the speech. Bill falls. I've replaced the packet.
Zofia: The title was read. It feels stupid that we can get that far and have to start again.
Grace: It would feel worse if you'd told them that meant passed.
Kit: First reading introduces it. There's more work after that. Mara said she'd seek time and support to bring it back. Seek. I checked the word with her.
Zofia: Niamh hasn't called.
Grace: Her mum might be talking to her.
Zofia: Right. Yes.
Kit: Shall I put the three undertakings behind your card? Then you can find each entry without turning everything over.
Zofia: Please. Put Hale last. He'll want to check the words.
Kit: Len, animals, Hale. Same cutoff. Different entries.
Grace: I'll carry the spare copies. Ask me if you need them.
Zofia: I will.
Kit: And this is mine. “NO SECOND CUE.” There.
Zofia: Leave a little space underneath. In case I need you to wait.
Kit: Zof.
Zofia: Just room for a word.
Kit: I can leave room for the names.
Zofia: Underneath them.
Kit: No.
